              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.458,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How Many Combinations"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.119,
            0.846,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Estu has 4 dresses and 3 caps. How many different ways can Estu combine the dresses and caps?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.163,
            0.848,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For each cap, he can choose any of the 4 dresses, so for 3 caps, 4 + 4 + 4 = 4 × 3 = 12 combinations are possible. We can also look at it as—for each dress, Estu can choose any of the 3 caps, so for 4 outfits, 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 3 × 4 = 12 combinations are possible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.241,
            0.846,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.157,
                0.241,
                0.846,
                0.408
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.415,
            0.847,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Roxie has 7 dresses, 2 hats, and 3 pairs of shoes. How many different ways can Roxie dress up?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.458,
            0.622,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Try drawing a diagram like the one above."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.489,
            0.794,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Estu and Roxie came across a safe containing old stamps and coins that their great-grandfather had collected. It was secured with a 5-digit password. Since nobody knew the password, they had no option except to try every password until it opened. They were unlucky and the lock only opened with the last password, after they had tried all possible combinations. How many passwords did they end up checking?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.803,
            0.463,
            0.89,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.803,
                0.463,
                0.89,
                0.604
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.639,
            0.185,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.104,
                0.639,
                0.185,
                0.695
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.64,
            0.825,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you can’t solve a problem, try to find a simpler version of the problem that you can solve. This technique can come in handy many times."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.715,
            0.846,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Instead of a 5-digit lock, let us assume we have a 2-digit lock and try to find out how many passwords are possible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.752,
            0.848,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are 10 options for the first digit (0 to 9). For each of these, there are 10 options for the second digit (If 0 is the first digit then 00, 01, 02, 03, …, 09 are possible). Therefore the total number of combinations for a 2-digit lock is 10 × 10 = 100."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.826,
            0.848,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, suppose we have a 3-digit lock. For each of the earlier 100 (2-digit) passwords there are 10 choices for the third digit. So, there are 100 × 10 = 1000 combinations for a 3-digit lock. You can list them all: 000, 001, 002, ….., 997, 997, 999."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.923,
            0.126,
            0.944
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2 6"
            }
          ]
        },
